---
description: "Dependency management — applies before adding, updating, or removing an npm package"
alwaysApply: false
---

Before adding a new package:
- Check it's actively maintained (recent commits/releases) and reasonably popular — avoid abandoned or single-maintainer-with-no-activity packages for anything load-bearing.
- Check the project doesn't already have something that does this job — don't add a second date library, a second HTTP client, a second form library, etc.
- Check its size/transitive dependency count is reasonable for what it's solving — don't pull in a heavy package for a one-line utility.
- Prefer well-known, ecosystem-standard choices over niche ones, all else equal.

When removing/updating: check nothing else in the codebase depends on the old behavior before bumping a major version.
